<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fondaco dei Tedeschi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Fondaco dei Tedeschi is a large building beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rialto">Rialto</a> Bridge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> that served from the 13th century as the headquarters, warehouse and lodging of German merchants, who were required to live and trade there under Venetian supervision. After a fire it was rebuilt in 1505–1508, with exterior frescoes by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giorgione">Giorgione</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titian">Titian</a>. It symbolised Venice's role as intermediary between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fondaco-dei-tedeschi.jpg" alt="Fondaco dei Tedeschi" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Didier Descouens · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fondaco_dei_Tedeschi_2012-05-13.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word fondaco comes from the Arabic funduq, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserai</a> combining warehouse, shop and inn for travelling merchants, a word also used for the lodgings of European merchants in eastern ports; Tedeschi means Germans. The first Fondaco was built in 1228 beside the Rialto Bridge on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a>. A German friar described it as having two central courtyards facing the canal, and a map by Jacopo de' Barbari shows it as many smaller buildings used for storage and housing. It also appears in the background of Vittore Carpaccio's painting Miracle of the Relic of the Cross at the Ponte di Rialto.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The building raised after the fire of 1505 is much larger, four floors set around one central courtyard, with fifty-six rooms and further storerooms; it could house over one hundred merchants, as many servants and a number of officials. The ground-floor storerooms were reached by water, the upper floors held living quarters and dining rooms, and a five-bay loggia on the canal received goods and merchants from boats. Its stairs resemble those of the Wakala al-Ghauri in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and its arches those of the Great Mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>. Of the frescoes only fragments survive, some kept at the Ca' d'Oro and the Gallerie dell'Accademia; others are known from prints in a folio by Antonio Maria Zanetti of the mid-18th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The merchants came from cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>, Judenburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>, and on arrival handed over their weapons to the Fontegher, the Venetian official who assigned the rooms. The Republic took a commission on the trade conducted there, which carried goods from the East toward the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. Germans occupied the building from shortly after it was built until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s arrival in 1806. In the 20th century it housed the headquarters of the Poste Italiane for about 70 years. Sold in 2008 to Edizione Property, a company of the Benetton family, for around 55 million euros, it was rebuilt from 2014 amid local protest and served as a luxury <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> store until the end of April 2025.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fondaco dei Tedeschi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…timber and slaves in the early Middle Ages and later became the principal intermediaries between western Europe and the eastern Mediterranean, importing spices, silk, cotton and alum from Constantinople, Alexandria, Beirut and the Black Sea, and selling them on through the <b>Fondaco dei Tedeschi</b> to merchants from Germany.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
